  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n=== Dry and Warm Through the End of the Week ===\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and weak flow aloft will bring \ncontinued dry and warm conditions through the end of this week.\n\nAmple mixing this afternoon will help drier air aloft to mix down to \nthe surface. Dew points are expected to be in the upper 30s and low \n40s and highs in the mid-upper 70s. Overnight and into Wednesday, \nhigh pressure will push off to the east and southwesterly flow will \nreturn. This will bring a warmer and slightly more moist airmass \ninto the region. Highs on Wednesday will be in the upper 70s and low \n80s. Though deep mixing in the afternoon will help dew points to \ndrop into the 40s and will bring breezy winds. \n\nThursday, a decaying cold front will move into the Ohio Valley and \nhigh pressure will build in behind. These features will reinforce \nthe dry airmass without much of a change in temperatures. We will \ncontinue to see highs in the upper 70s and low 80s through the end \nof the week. \n\n=== Weekend: Remnants of a Potential Tropical System ===\n\nOn Saturday, high pressure will shift east of the region and \nsoutherly return flow will increase temperatures and moisture over \nthe region. Aloft, ridging will quickly build and enhance increased \nhigh temperatures. \n\nThe National Hurricane Center has outlined AL92 in the Gulf with an \n80% chance of becoming a tropical cyclone over the next few days. \nWhile there is still uncertainty in the strength of this system and \nthe interaction with the strong ridging over the Mid-Atlantic / Ohio \nValley, this system may bring remnant energy and moisture into the \nlower Ohio Valley. If the low pressure tracks north into the lower \nOhio Valley, we will expect to see plenty of moisture and beneficial \nrainfall. There is still quite a bit of spread in QPF totals, since \nit is conditional on the track of the tropical system. Though, there \nis a 45% chance of exceeding 1 inch of rainfall and a 20% chance of \nexceeding 2 inches over southern Kentucky. Over the next few \nforecast cycles, more details will become clearer.",
